# Common classes for modeling and diagramsss

:ModelConcept
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "ModelConcept" ;
    skos:definition '''
ModelConcept is the base Class for all Concepts used in an Architecture [Model](#Model), namely:
* [Element](#Element)
* [QualifiedRelationship](#QualifiedRelationship)
* [View](#View)

More specific type of construct can be defined by extending this Class. Usually one will not need to use this Class
directly, but mostly each of the subclasses. Custom Model Concepts are defined in the [Metamodel](#Metamodel).

The palette of available concepts for a modeling tool is built from subclasses of Element (for nodes) and
subclasses of QualifiedRelationship (for edges). Both carry visual notations, taxonomy classifications,
and domain/range guidance.
        ''' ;
    :rationale      '''
To understand why we have ModelConcepts base Class and why not use directly Concepts or Classes, we first need to
understand what does Architecture Model mean in our context. That it is something mainly used to address stakeholder
concerns also used to help in and possibly record the decision processes. This means that all those Model Concepts needs
 to be presented in different context, have specific visual notation according to that context and understood
 correctly by stakeholders. This
 involves lots of visual communication and contextual data. Model concepts are also used in the architecture modelling
 processes and this requires that there is a repository of predefined Concepts that an Architect can use in the Model.
 There are also certain restrictions that applies to those modelConcepts which are specified in the Architecture
 framework used and modelled in the :Metamodel(#Metamodel).
    ''' ;
.

:Element
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :ModelConcept ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Element"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
Element is the class for Elements or Nodes used in models.
Element sub-classes are to be defined in more specific Architecture Meta Models
Element instances are to be used in the actual Architecture Models.
In general an Architecture Element represents any significant component of the architecture that needs to be addressed.
        '''
.

:QualifiedRelationship
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :ModelConcept ;
    skos:prefLabel  "QualifiedRelationship" ;
    skos:definition '''
A first-class architectural resource that represents a named, addressable relationship occurrence between two
[ModelConcept](#ModelConcept) resources.

QualifiedRelationship instances are genuine architectural objects — they have identity, lifecycle, provenance,
governance state, and can themselves be the source or target of other relationships (higher-order modeling).

Each QualifiedRelationship carries:
* [source](#source) — the source element
* [target](#target) — the target element

The unqualified predicate is determined from:
* the class definition via [unqualifiedForm](#unqualifiedForm) (schema-level)
* the reified triple via rdf:reifies (instance-level, RDF 1.2)

Three complementary representations are emitted for every relationship:
1. **Direct triple** — `source vocab:serves target` — for SPARQL traversal
2. **Qualified predicate** — `source vocab:qualifiedServing relationshipNode` — to navigate to the full resource;
   named after the class it leads to, as PROV-O names `prov:qualifiedUsage` after `prov:Usage`
3. **Qualified relationship resource** (this class) — carries all metadata: labels, provenance, lifecycle, etc.
4. **rdf:reifies** — bridges the direct triple to this resource (RDF 1.2)

The relationship resource is the canonical form: converters and modelling tools write one for every relationship,
whether or not it carries metadata yet, and the questions a model is asked about its relationships read it. A
graph trimmed to the direct triples, for fast traversal, is a projection of the model: it answers which elements
are connected, not what is recorded about a connection, nor the questions that read relationship resources.

A relationship resource whose triple is not asserted is a proposition, not a relationship of the model:
rdf:reifies names a triple without asserting it. A potential derivation is one, until the modeller confirms it:
asserting the triple, with its qualified predicate, confirms a proposition, and its resource then records that it
was derived. A valid derivation is a statement: a rule asserts its triple, with its qualified predicate, and its
resource records how. What a modeller stated is told from what a rule derived by the graph, in a store, and by the
resource.

With RDF 1.2, `rdf:reifies` bridges the direct triple to this resource, replacing the older rdf:Statement approach.
Multiple distinct reifiers for the same proposition are supported — essential when the same logical relation appears
in different contexts (current vs target state, observed vs inferred, CMDB-discovered vs design-reviewed).

Subclasses of QualifiedRelationship are defined in the metamodel for each specific relationship type.
The schema-level mapping to the unqualified predicate is declared using [unqualifiedForm](#unqualifiedForm).
The generic fallback predicate [hasQualifiedRelationship](#hasQualifiedRelationship) is used when no
type-specific qualified predicate is mapped.
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''
This class adopts the PROV-O qualified relation pattern (prov:qualifiedInfluence / prov:EntityInfluence)
and extends it for architecture modeling. The mapping is:

  PROV-O                          Linked.Archi
  ─────                           ───────────
  prov:EntityInfluence            arch:QualifiedRelationship
  prov:influencer / prov:entity   arch:source / arch:target
  prov:unqualifiedForm            arch:unqualifiedForm (schema-level)
  prov:qualifiedInfluence         type-specific qualified property (e.g. am:qualifiedServing)
  (no equivalent)                 rdf:reifies (RDF 1.2 — bridges direct triple to resource)

With RDF 1.2, rdf:reifies provides the standardized bridge between the direct triple and this resource,
replacing the older rdf:Statement approach.

Instances of QualifiedRelationship are not reification workarounds — they are managed architectural objects
that support lifecycle (proposed → approved → deprecated), governance (ownership, approval), and higher-order
links (a risk assessment targeting a data flow).
    ''' ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/#qualifiedForms>,
                    <https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-concepts/>,
                    <https://patterns.dataincubator.org/book/qualified-relation.html> ;
    skos:closeMatch prov:EntityInfluence ;
    skos:example    '''
# Metamodel: define the relationship type
am:serves
    a                    owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel       "Serves"@en ;
    arch:domainIncludes  am:ApplicationService ;
    arch:rangeIncludes   am:BusinessProcess ;
.

am:Serving
    a                    owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf      arch:QualifiedRelationship ;
    arch:unqualifiedForm am:serves ;
    skos:prefLabel       "Serving"@en ;
    arch:prefVisNotation "https://ex.com/serves.svg" ;
.

am:qualifiedServing
    a                    owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range           am:Serving ;
    arch:unqualifiedForm am:serves ;
.

# Model data: emit both representations
ex:AppSvc1 am:serves ex:BizProc1 .

ex:AppSvc1 am:qualifiedServing ex:qr-005 .
ex:qr-005 a am:Serving ;
    arch:source ex:AppSvc1 ;
    arch:target ex:BizProc1 ;
    rdf:reifies <<( ex:AppSvc1 am:serves ex:BizProc1 )>> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom ex:diagramEdge-81 .
''' ;
    skos:historyNote '''
Earlier versions of this ontology had a separate :Relationship class (rdfs:subClassOf :ModelConcept, rdf:Property)
using a metaclass pattern where instances were automatically rdf:Property. That class has been removed.
Relationship types are now standard owl:ObjectProperty. QualifiedRelationship subclasses serve as the palette
concepts for relationship types (symmetric with Element subclasses for element types).

The earlier design also based QualifiedRelationship on rdf:Statement with relSource/relTarget/relPredicate as
subproperties of rdf:subject/rdf:object/rdf:predicate. That dependency on old-style reification has been removed.
RDF 1.2 rdf:reifies is the intended bridge between the direct triple and the relationship resource.
''' ;
.

:hasQualifiedRelationship
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "hasQualifiedRelationship" ;
    skos:definition "Generic fallback qualified predicate used when no type-specific qualified predicate is mapped for a relationship type. Points from a source element to a QualifiedRelationship node." ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range      :QualifiedRelationship ;
.

:unqualifiedForm
    a               owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "unqualifiedForm" ;
    skos:definition '''Schema-level mapping: declares which unqualified predicate a qualified form
(qualified property or QualifiedRelationship subclass) corresponds to.

Use on schema definitions only, not on instance data. At the instance level, the unqualified
predicate is available via rdf:reifies on the QualifiedRelationship occurrence (RDF 1.2).

Example:
  am:qualifiedServing arch:unqualifiedForm am:serves .
  am:Serving        arch:unqualifiedForm am:serves .
''' ;
    skos:closeMatch prov:unqualifiedForm ;
.

:Viewpoint
    a                         owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel            "Viewpoint" ;
    skos:definition           '''
    An architecture Viewpoint is a specification of the conventions for a particular kind of architecture view.
    It can also be called the definition or schema for that kind of architecture view. It establishes the conventions
    for constructing, interpreting, and using an architecture view to address a specific concern (or set of concerns)
    about a system-of-interest.
    A viewpoint can be seen as a diferent perspective of the subject area that shapes the Metamodel, which defines
    the Model.
    Viewpoints may look quite different so as to be meaningful to different stakeholders
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010: "a work product establishing the conventions for the construction, interpretation and use of architecture views to frame specific system concerns." Also aligned with ArchiMate Chapter 14 and TOGAF viewpoint definitions.'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html>,
                    <https://pubs.opengroup.org/architecture/archimate3-doc/ch-Stakeholders-Architecture-Views-and-Viewpoints.html>,
                    <https://iasa-global.github.io/btabok/views.html> ;
    prov:qualifiedAttribution :dd ;
.

:View
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :ModelConcept ;
    skos:prefLabel  "View" ;
    skos:definition '''
    Architecture View is a representation of a system from the perspective of a related set of concerns.
    It consists of one or more architecture models of the system
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Maps to ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 "Architecture View" — a representation of a system from the perspective of a related set of concerns. A view is governed by its viewpoint. Subtypes Catalog, Matrix, and Diagram align with TOGAF artifact classification.'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html>,
                    <https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap03.html> ;
.

:Catalog
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :View ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Catalog" ;
    skos:definition ''' Catalog is a list of building blocks of a specific type, or of related types,
    that are used for governance or reference purposes (for example, an organization chart, showing
    locations and actors). As with building blocks, catalogs carry metadata according to the metamodel, which supports
    query and analysis.
        ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Aligned with TOGAF Architecture Content Framework: "a list of building blocks of a specific type, or of related types, that are used for governance or reference purposes." See TOGAF 10 Architecture Content, Chapter 3.'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html>,
                    <https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap03.html> ;
.

:Matrix
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :View ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Matrix" ;
    skos:definition '''Matrix is a grid that shows relationships between two or more model entities. Matrices are used
        to represent relationships that are list-based rather than graphical in their usage (for example, a CRUD
        matrix showing which applications Create, Read, Update, and Delete a particular type of data is difficult to
        represent visually).
        ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Aligned with TOGAF Architecture Content Framework: "a grid that shows relationships between two or more model entities." See TOGAF 10 Architecture Content, Chapter 3.'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html>,
                    <https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap03.html> ;
.

:Diagram
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :View ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Diagram" ;
    skos:definition '''Diagrams are renderings of architectural content in a graphical format to allow stakeholders to
    retrieve the required information. Diagrams can also be used as a technique for graphically populating architecture
    content or for checking the completeness of information that has been collected.
        The TOGAF content framework defines a set of architecture diagrams to be created (e.g., organization chart).
        Each of these diagrams may be created several times for an architecture with different style or content coverage
         to suit stakeholder concerns.
         ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Aligned with TOGAF Architecture Content Framework: "renderings of architectural content in a graphical format to allow stakeholders to retrieve the required information." See TOGAF 10 Architecture Content, Chapter 3.'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html>,
                    <https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap03.html> ;
.

:Model
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Model" ;
    skos:definition '''
    A model is a selective representation of some system whose form
    and content are chosen based on a specific set of concerns. The
    model is related to the system by an explicit or implicit mapping.

    ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 <b>Architecture Description</b> is an example of a Model, which can be a simple model
    or an aggregate of multiple models from different domains.

    The scope and context of a Model is established by analysing the domain subject area and defined by the
    Metamodel.

    High-level domains may include:
    * Telecommunications
    * Healthcare
    * Retail
    * Manufacturing
    * Transportation
    * Defense
    * Government

    More precise domains within a particular organization may be related to the different organizational
    bodies and depertments such as
    * Enterprose Architecture
    * Solution Architecture
    * Security Architecture,
    * Software Engineering
    * Operational Services
    * Business Departments...

    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Maps to ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 "Architecture Description" — a work product used to express the architecture of some entity of interest. In Linked.Archi, Model is a more general term that encompasses architecture descriptions and other selective representations.

Model carries both standards' aggregate role: it is the ISO 42010 Architecture Description,
comprised of one or more Architecture Views, and the TOGAF deliverable — an Architecture
Definition Document and the like — that aggregates catalogs, matrices and diagrams. Membership is
stated by the member: a View names its Model with arch:inModel.'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html>,
                    <https://eaonapage.com/> ;
.

:Metamodel
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Metamodel" ;
    skos:definition '''
    A Metamodel in Linked.Archi context is the definition (Model) of a Modeling Language, so that
    models can be expressed in a way that communicates information about a system among involved
    stakeholders and can be correctly interpreted by those stakeholders and supporting technologies.
    The Modeling Language formalizes the structure, terms, notations, syntax, semantics, integrity
    rules, inference/derivation rules, and also a set of standard Viewpoints.

    Well-known modeling languages that can be formalized as Linked.Archi metamodels include
    UML, BPMN, ArchiMate, C4, E/R, and SQL Schema. These are domain-level languages whose
    concepts become arch:Element and arch:QualifiedRelationship subclasses in the knowledge graph.

    Note: RDF/OWL/SHACL/SKOS is the representation framework in which all the above modeling
    languages are expressed — it operates at the meta-meta level, not as a peer of UML or BPMN.
    Listing OWL alongside UML as a "modeling language" is technically defensible (OWL can model
    domains), but in the Linked.Archi context OWL is the substrate, not a sibling. The OMG
    Ontology Definition Metamodel (ODM, formal/2014-09-02) explicitly defines the mapping between
    MOF/UML and OWL/RDF, confirming that RDF/OWL is the layer below MOF-based languages that
    enables their semantic integration.

    A model is said to conform to a modeling language.

    ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 Architecture Description can be based on a specific metamodel, which can
    be a simple metamodel or an aggregate of multiple metamodels from different domains.

    A Metamodel is a manifest that ties together all constituent resources: the model concepts
    ontology, SHACL shapes, derivation rules, viewpoints, taxonomy, deliverable templates, and
    other artifacts. It is the entry point for tools that need to discover everything that makes
    up a modeling language.
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Maps to ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 "Architecture Description Language" — a language for use in
architecture descriptions. In Linked.Archi, a Metamodel aggregates OWL ontologies, SHACL shapes,
SKOS schemes, and other resources that together define the vocabulary and rules for a modeling
language. In Linked.Archi, RDFS/OWL is the foundational layer (analogous to M3 in the OMG
four-layer architecture). Modeling languages like UML, BPMN, and ArchiMate are expressed as
OWL ontologies (M2), and model instances are RDF individuals typed with those ontology
classes (M1). The OMG four-layer framing is used here for didactic continuity with
OMG-trained colleagues, not because MOF is structurally necessary — RDFS is reflective
and self-describing without a separate meta-meta tier.'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html>,
                    <https://www.omg.org/spec/ODM/>,
                    <https://www.omg.org/spec/MOF/> ;
.

:Architecture
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Architecture" ;
    skos:definition '''
    fundamental concepts or properties related to a :System in its environment embodied in its elements, relationships,
    and in the principles of its design and evolution.
    Architecture is different form Architecture description as Specified in ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 Standard. Architecture
     is an abstract Concept that maps directly to a System of in other words Entity of Interest. Any System has an
     Architecture whether intentional or accidental, known or unknown, documented or contained as tacit knowledge.
     Architecture Description (see ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010) is a work product that expresses the architecture. In Linked
     .Archi 
     context :Model is a more formal specialization of Architecture Description.
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010: "fundamental concepts or properties of a system in its environment embodied in its elements, relationships, and in the principles of its design and evolution." The standard distinguishes Architecture (inherent to a system) from Architecture Description (a work product).'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html> ;
.

:Framework
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf schema:CreativeWork ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Framework" ;
    skos:definition '''
    Framework in Linked.Archi context is used to represent any type of Architecture framework. A Metamodel could be
    defined within a framework and thus part of it example Togaf Content Metamodel or OAF which is mostly a
    MetaModel or Zachman Framework even an Ontology. Frameworks may define other concepts like Processes, which is
    the case with Togaf ADM.

    Framework is characterized with ... check is standard...
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Maps to ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 "Architecture Framework" — conventions, principles, and practices for architecture description established within a specific domain or community. Examples: TOGAF, Zachman, ADMIT.'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html>,
                    <https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/> ;
.


# Define framewrok componenets?

:System
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "System" ;
    skos:definition '''
The subject of interest of a Model that is a combination of interrelated and interdependent parts organized to
achieve one or more stated purposes. As such a system is defined by its structure and purpose.

A system is influenced by its environment and may be bounded by space and time.
This is inclusive of designs at all levels such as an entire enterprise, a process, information structures
or I.T. systems.
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Maps to ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 "Entity of Interest" (2022 edition) or "System of Interest" (2011 edition). The standard uses "system" as a placeholder that can refer to an enterprise, system of systems, product line, service, subsystem, or software.'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html> ;
.

:Purpose
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Purpose" ;
    skos:definition '''
As a work product, an AD is devised for the specific purpose for which the architecting effort is undertaken.
This purpose shapes the AD and also act as force in the architecting effort. This is the same as "Design
Objectives" mentioned in ADD 3.0.
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Related to ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 concept of the purpose for which an architecture description is devised. Also related to ADD 3.0 "Design Objectives."'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''For a presales proposal architecture work involves rapid design. Here the main viewpoint would
        be high level structure and behaviour as well as cost estimations. The design work will not go in details'''
.

:Environment
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Environment" ;
    skos:definition '''The context in which a system exists, including external entities, conditions, and influences that interact with or affect the system. Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010.'''@en ;
.

:Stakeholder
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :Element ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Stakeholder" ;
    skos:definition '''
    :Stakeholder is the class for Stakeholders used in describing high level architecture interest.
    Stakeholder is a first-class element and represented in the Code ontology as it affects directly the way the
    architecture and  architecture description will be organized and thus impact the MetaModel.
    Stakeholder can be used also as a :ModelConcept and for that :Element can be extended. Same instance of
    :Stakeholder and :element can be used and this also recommended as will benefit the analysis and inference.
Example Types of stakeholders:
a) Users of the system
b) Acquirers of the system
c) Developers of the system
d) Maintainers of the system
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010: "individual, team, or organization holding concerns for the system of interest." Also aligned with ArchiMate Stakeholder element and TOGAF stakeholder management.'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html>,
                    <https://iasa-global.github.io/btabok/stakeholders.html> ;
.



:Consideration
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Consideration" ;
    skos:definition '''Abstract base class for concerns, aspects, and perspectives — the interests that shape how architecture is described and communicated. Subclasses: Concern, Aspect, Perspective.'''@en ;
.

:Concern
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :Consideration ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Concern" ;
    skos:definition '''

Example concerns:
— The purpose or missions of the system
— The appropriateness of the system for use in fulfilling its missions
— The feasibility of constructing the system
— The risks of system development and operation to users, acquirers, and developers of the system
— Maintainability, deployability, and evolvability of the system
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010: "any interest in the system." The term derives from "separation of concerns" (Dijkstra). Examples include purpose, functionality, structure, behavior, cost, supportability, safety, interoperability.

A concern is about the system and is named by a noun phrase ("Impact of change"). It is not answered
by any one question: a ModelQuestion asks one thing the model answers, and informs the concerns it
addresses (arch:questionAddressesConcern).'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html> ;
.

:Aspect
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :Consideration ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Aspect" ;
    skos:definition '''
    Architecture Aspect
    example:
    data, activity, function, location, people, time, motivation, taxonomy, structure, connectivity, behaviour,
    information, parameters, constraints, roadmaps, traceability, requirements
    ''' ;
.

:Perspective
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :Consideration ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Perspective" ;
    skos:definition '''
Stakeholder Perspective.
    Example:
* strategic
* capability
* operations
* services
* physical
    ''' ;
    skos:example    '''
* strategic
* capability
* operations
* services
* physical
'''
.

:PresentationFormat
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "PresentationFormat"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Defines the output format for rendering a deliverable — e.g., Markdown,
HTML, PDF, slides, dashboard. A DeliverableTemplate specifies which formats
it supports via arch:templateHasFormat.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''
* Markdown
* HTML
* PDF
* Slide Deck (PPT)
* Interactive Dashboard
'''@en ;
    skos:historyNote '''Replaces the earlier arch:PresentationTemplate class, which conflated
content structure (what viewpoints to include) with rendering format (how to present).
The content structure role is now handled by arch:DeliverableTemplate. The rendering
format role is handled by this class.'''@en ;
.

:DeliverableTemplate
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf schema:CreativeWork ;
    skos:prefLabel  "DeliverableTemplate"@en ;
    skos:definition '''A template of an architecture document produced from a model: its sections in
order, each presenting a viewpoint and answering concerns by asking model questions; the values the
document is produced for (arch:templateParameter); and the presentation formats it can be rendered in.
A generator produces the document from the model, so it states what the model holds, what it lacks
and what could not be checked, and does not drift from the model.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Aligned with TOGAF Architecture Content Framework where deliverables
(e.g., Architecture Definition Document, Architecture Requirements Specification) are
defined as aggregations of specific catalogs, matrices, and diagrams. Also aligned with
ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 where an Architecture Description is comprised of Architecture Views,
each governed by a Viewpoint.

DeliverableTemplates are defined at the Metamodel level via arch:hasDeliverableTemplate,
alongside other metamodel components (elements, relationships, viewpoints, rules).
A Model names the templates its deliverables follow with arch:deliverableTemplateUsed.

Example, abridged from the ArchiMate 4.0 templates:
  am4dt:ArchitectureDefinitionDocument a arch:DeliverableTemplate ;
      arch:templateParameter am4dt:ModelParameter ;
      arch:templateHasSection am4dt:ADD-S1, am4dt:ADD-S2 ;
      arch:templateRequiresViewpoint am4vp:OrganizationViewpoint, am4vp:ProcessCooperationViewpoint ;
      arch:templateHasFormat archdt:MarkdownFormat, archdt:PDFFormat ;
      arch:templateTargetsPurpose archvp:Designing .

The document presents the model's views by the viewpoint each follows. Each View names the
Model it belongs to — membership is stated by the member, not the container. See arch:inModel.
  ex:Claims a arch:Model ;
      arch:deliverableTemplateUsed am4dt:ArchitectureDefinitionDocument .
  ex:ProcessView a arch:Diagram ;
      arch:inModel ex:Claims ;
      arch:viewConformsToViewpoint am4vp:ProcessCooperationViewpoint .
'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html>,
                    <https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap03.html> ;
.

:TemplateSection
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "TemplateSection"@en ;
    skos:definition '''An ordered section of a DeliverableTemplate: the Viewpoint it presents
(arch:sectionViewpoint), the concerns it answers (arch:sectionAddressesConcern) and the model
questions it asks to answer them (arch:sectionAsks), or a single inline query (arch:sectionQuery).
Produced from a model, the section shows the answers and the model's views that follow its
viewpoint.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''
am4dt:ADD-S2 a arch:TemplateSection ;
    arch:sectionOrder 2 ;
    skos:prefLabel "2. Process Cooperation"@en ;
    skos:definition "How do processes cooperate, is each performed by someone, and is every service delivered?"@en ;
    arch:sectionViewpoint am4vp:ProcessCooperationViewpoint ;
    arch:sectionAddressesConcern am4vp:ProcessDependencies, am4vp:Responsibilities, am4vp:Completeness ;
    arch:sectionAsks ( am4q:viewpoint-scope am4q:unassigned-behaviour am4q:unrealised-services ) ;
    arch:sectionResultKey "processCooperation" .
'''@en ;
.

## ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
## Model questions — what stakeholders ask of a model
##
## A Concern is an interest a stakeholder holds in the system; a ModelQuestion is
## a question the model — the architecture description — answers, with the
## SPARQL that answers it. A concern is informed by many questions, and by views;
## a question informs the concerns it addresses. The question is bound to the
## same ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 terms viewpoints and deliverable templates are
## described with: defined once, it is offered by a modelling tool, asked by a
## deliverable section and found by an analysis agent through its concerns.
##
## The executable part is SHACL Advanced Features' sh:SPARQLExecutable: the
## query in sh:select, sh:construct or sh:ask, its prefixes declared once through
## sh:prefixes / sh:declare, its inputs declared with sh:parameter.
## ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

:ModelQuestion
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sh:SPARQLExecutable ;
    skos:prefLabel  "ModelQuestion"@en ;
    skos:altLabel   "Question"@en, "Competency question"@en, "Model query"@en ;
    skos:definition '''A question an architecture model answers — "What is affected if this element
changes?" — together with the executable SPARQL that answers it. Where a Concern is an interest a
stakeholder holds in the system, such as the impact of change, a model question asks one thing the
architecture description can answer, and informs the concerns it addresses
(arch:questionAddressesConcern): a concern is informed by many questions and views, and no single
answer settles it. The question is the unit of reuse: a modelling tool offers it beside the views it
fits, a deliverable section asks it for the concerns it answers, and an analysis agent selects it by
those concerns.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Wording. The skos:prefLabel is the question itself, one sentence ending in a
question mark; a Concern's is a noun phrase. arch:doesNotProve states what the model's answer does
not settle about the system.

Executable form. An instance is also typed sh:SPARQLSelectExecutable,
sh:SPARQLConstructExecutable or sh:SPARQLAskExecutable and carries its query in sh:select,
sh:construct or sh:ask. The query text carries no PREFIX declarations — they are named through
sh:prefixes, normally the question set's root resource with its sh:declare entries — and no
LIMIT: arch:defaultLimit is the bound a browsing tool appends, while a document generator reads
every row.

Parameters. Declared with sh:parameter; as in SHACL, the variable a parameter binds is the
local name of its sh:path, so a parameter on arch:focus binds ?focus. The core parameter
properties are arch:focus, arch:model, arch:view, arch:viewpoint, arch:conceptType, arch:from
and arch:to. A runner pre-binds a parameter by inserting "VALUES ?focus { <iri> }" as the first
element of the outermost WHERE group, and binds only the parameters the question declares: a
result column may share a parameter's name without being one. An optional parameter left
unbound stays a free variable, so the stored query is valid SPARQL that runs unchanged in any
tool and answers for "any"; a question whose required parameter is unbound is not asked. A
sub-SELECT that uses a parameter's variable projects it, so the pre-binding still applies to its
rows.

Dataset. Model data is in the default graph. A question that also reads the metamodel (a
subclass closure, the concepts a viewpoint admits) reads it through
"{ P } UNION { GRAPH ?g { P } }", which holds whether the metamodel is in the default graph or
in one named graph per document; arch:requiresDataset says which a question needs. Such a
lookup is made once per query — a sub-SELECT of the types concerned, joined or subtracted —
not once per row.

Types. A concept's type is its most specific one. The core classes (arch:ModelConcept,
arch:Element, arch:QualifiedRelationship) and a language's abstract classes may be asserted or
inferred beside it, and a question's answer does not change when they are: a question that
reports or groups by type leaves them out.

Findings. When arch:resultIsFinding is true, each row is a problem against the concerns the
question addresses, so an empty result means they are satisfied; arch:findingSeverity grades
the rows on SHACL's scale and arch:findingFocus names the column holding the concept each
finding is about, so findings read like validation results.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''
ex:orphans a arch:ModelQuestion, sh:SPARQLSelectExecutable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Which elements take part in no relationship?"@en ;
    arch:questionStage arch:QualityStage ;
    arch:resultIsFinding true ; arch:findingSeverity sh:Info ; arch:findingFocus "element" ;
    sh:parameter [ sh:path arch:model ; sh:optional true ] ;
    sh:prefixes <https://example.org/questions#> ;
    sh:select """
        SELECT ?element ?label WHERE {
          ?element arch:inModel ?model .
          FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?element arch:source ?anySource }
          FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?anyRelationship arch:source|arch:target ?element }
          OPTIONAL { ?element skos:prefLabel ?label }
        }""" .
'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.w3.org/TR/shacl-af/#SPARQLExecutable>,
                    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html> ;
.

:QuestionSet
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "QuestionSet"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The ModelQuestions a language publishes, as one document: the core set, or a
metamodel's set for its own vocabulary. A unit of publication, not a grouping by concern — the
questions that inform a concern are found through arch:questionAddressesConcern. Its root resource
carries the document's metadata (title, licence, vann:preferredNamespaceUri and
vann:preferredNamespacePrefix) and declares, with sh:declare, the prefixes its questions' SPARQL
uses; each question names it with sh:prefixes.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''A question set is instance data, like a set of deliverable templates: its
root is an arch:QuestionSet, never an owl:Ontology. A metamodel links the question sets for its
vocabulary with arch:hasQuestionSet.'''@en ;
.

:DatasetScope
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "DatasetScope"@en ;
    skos:definition "What a question reads: model data alone, or model data with the metamodel beside it."@en ;
.

:ModelDataset
    a               :DatasetScope ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Model data"@en ;
    skos:definition "Model data only, in the default graph."@en ;
.

:ModelAndMetamodelDataset
    a               :DatasetScope ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Model and metamodel"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Model data together with the loaded metamodel documents — class hierarchy,
viewpoint definitions, unqualified forms — in the default graph or in named graphs.'''@en ;
.

:QuestionStageScheme
    a               skos:ConceptScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Question stages"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The stages an investigation of a model moves through, in order: what is
there, what a name refers to, which vocabulary the data uses, how things depend on each other,
what is recorded about them, how sound the description is, and what its views show.'''@en ;
.

:OrientationStage
    a               skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme   :QuestionStageScheme ;
    skos:notation   "orientation" ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Orientation"@en ;
    skos:definition "What is in the model, in outline."@en ;
.

:ResolutionStage
    a               skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme   :QuestionStageScheme ;
    skos:notation   "resolution" ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Resolution"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which concept a name refers to, and what it is."@en ;
.

:DiscoveryStage
    a               skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme   :QuestionStageScheme ;
    skos:notation   "discovery" ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Discovery"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which vocabulary and shapes the data actually uses."@en ;
.

:AnalysisStage
    a               skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme   :QuestionStageScheme ;
    skos:notation   "analysis" ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Analysis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Dependencies, traceability, realisation and the impact of change."@en ;
.

:EnrichmentStage
    a               skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme   :QuestionStageScheme ;
    skos:notation   "enrichment" ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Enrichment"@en ;
    skos:definition "Lifecycle, ownership and the other facts recorded about concepts."@en ;
.

:QualityStage
    a               skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme   :QuestionStageScheme ;
    skos:notation   "quality" ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Quality"@en ;
    skos:definition "Gaps, inconsistencies and the integrity of the description itself."@en ;
.

:ViewsStage
    a               skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme   :QuestionStageScheme ;
    skos:notation   "views" ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Views"@en ;
    skos:definition "What views present, and how they conform to their viewpoints."@en ;
.

:QualityAttribute
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "QualityAttribute" ;
    skos:definition '''
Quality attributes are characteristics of a Software Product Quality, Software in Use Quality, Data Quality, Service
Quality, Enterprise Quality, or any other System Quality.
An instance of QualityAttribute class represent inherent property or characteristic of an Element or
QualityAttributeRequirement so that it can be distinguished quantitatively or qualitatively.

Quality Attribute is quantified by [QualityMeasure](#QualityMeasure) that can be related to an Element or
QualityAttributeRequirement. Element in that case can be a  work product or behavior of a system, software, or
stakeholders such as users, operators, developers, testers, or maintainers.


    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Aligned with ISO/IEC 25010 (Systems and software quality models) and the SEI/CMU quality attribute characterization. Quality attributes are key architectural forces in the ADD (Attribute-Driven Design) method.'''@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/35733.html>,
                    <https://www.sebokwiki.org/wiki/Quality_Management#Quality_Attributes>,
                    <https://iasa-global.github.io/btabok/quality_attributes.html>,
                    <https://iasa-global.github.io/btabok/boqa.html> ;
    :mentionedIn    <https://www.sebokwiki.org/wiki/Quality_Management#Quality_Attributes> ;
.

# folders

:Folder
    a               owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf schema:ItemList ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Folder"@en ;
    skos:definition "UI/navigation container used to preserve tool-native organization (containment + stable display order). Not a domain/architecture semantic construct."@en .

## Governance
:conceptOwner
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain        :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range         :Stakeholder ;
    skos:prefLabel     "Concept Owner"@en ;
    skos:definition    '''
        Indicates the stakeholder or governance body responsible for the maintenance and governance of an
        architecture concept within the architecture repository.
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote     '''
        This property helps to clearly identify accountability and stewardship, facilitating effective governance and
         lifecycle management of architecture elements.
    ''' ;
    skos:editorialNote '''
        Use this property to explicitly document ownership to avoid ambiguity in governance responsibilities across
        various organisational units or roles.
    ''' ;
.

:masterDataSource
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain        :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range         dcat:Dataset ;
    skos:prefLabel     "Master Data Source"@en ;
    skos:definition    '''
        Specifies the authoritative data source or primary repository from which the architecture element's data is
        maintained, exported, or synchronised.
    ''' ;
    skos:scopeNote     '''
        This annotation is particularly crucial for scenarios involving data aggregation, ensuring traceability and
        consistency across diverse architecture modelling and management tools.
    ''' ;
    skos:editorialNote '''
        Maintain clarity in source identification to enhance data reliability and simplify maintenance and
        integration workflows within the architecture repository.
    ''' ;
.

## << Governance

# to give examples with provo and measurement method& function is separate ontology - ex: quality measurement and
# evaluation include parts of iso2502/3/4 + 42030 and part of 42020
:QualityMeasure
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "QualityMeasure" ;
    skos:definition '''
Used to quantify QualityAttributes
''' ;
    :mentionedIn    <https://www.sebokwiki.org/wiki/Quality_Management#Quality_Attributes> ;
.

# Properties
## Quality attribute related
:hasQualityMeasure
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf   :measuredEntity ;
    skos:prefLabel  "hasQualityMeasure"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
Whether an element has a quality attribute measure associated to it.
A domain can be an Element such as work product or behavior of a system, software, or
stakeholders such as users, operators, developers, testers, or maintainers.

'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Element ;
    rdfs:range      :QualityMeasure ;
.

:measuredQualityAttribute
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "measuredQualityAttribute"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a QualityMeasure to the QualityAttribute it quantifies.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :QualityMeasure ;
    rdfs:range      :QualityAttribute ;
.

:measuredEntity
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf   :hasQualityMeasure ;
    skos:prefLabel  "measuredEntity"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a QualityMeasure to the Element it measures.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :QualityMeasure ;
    rdfs:range      :Element ;
.

## QualifiedRelationship props
:source
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel     "source" ;
    skos:altLabel      "relationship source" ;
    skos:definition    "the source element of a qualified relationship" ;
    skos:scopeNote     '''Analogous to the influencer/agent entity in a PROV-O qualified influence.
In the PROV-O pattern, a qualified node points back to the entity that caused the influence;
here, source points to the element from which the relationship originates.''' ;
    rdfs:domain        :QualifiedRelationship ;
    rdfs:range         :ModelConcept ;
.

:target
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel     "target" ;
    skos:altLabel      "relationship target" ;
    skos:definition    "the target element of a qualified relationship" ;
    skos:scopeNote     '''Analogous to the influenced entity in a PROV-O qualified influence.
In the PROV-O pattern, the qualified node is reached from the influenced entity;
here, target points to the element at which the relationship arrives.''' ;
    rdfs:domain        :QualifiedRelationship ;
    rdfs:range         :ModelConcept ;
.

:domainIncludes
    a               owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "domainIncludes" ;
    skos:definition "The classes a property's subject is expected to be. Guidance, not a constraint: not used for validation."@en ;
    skos:closeMatch schema:domainIncludes ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''On a relationship's predicate (am4:serves), the classes of its source element; on an order of
proposal a Metamodel names (:relationshipProposalOrder), the sources it applies to. Unlike rdfs:domain it infers
nothing about what uses the property: it guides modelling tools and readers, and restricts nothing. Restrictions, as
all constraints, are SHACL's.'''@en ;
.

:rangeIncludes
    a               owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "rangeIncludes" ;
    skos:definition "The classes a property's object is expected to be. Guidance, not a constraint: not used for validation."@en ;
    skos:closeMatch schema:rangeIncludes ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''On a relationship's predicate (am4:serves), the classes of its target element; on an order of
proposal a Metamodel names (:relationshipProposalOrder), the targets it applies to. Unlike rdfs:range it infers
nothing about the values: it guides modelling tools and readers, and restricts nothing. Restrictions, as all
constraints, are SHACL's.'''@en ;
.


## Basic Properties


## use dcterms:source for ontology terms, prov:hadPrimarySource for individuals or seealso for week connections
#:specURL
#    a                  owl:AnnotationProperty ;
#    rdfs:subPropertyOf schema:url ;
#    skos:prefLabel         "specURL" ;
#    skos:definition       "The URL of the official specification"
##    rdfs:domain        :ModelConcept ;
#.

:basedOnFramework
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf prov:hadPrimarySource ;
    skos:prefLabel     "definedByFramework" ;
    skos:definition    "The :Framework that is used as a foundation for the :MetaModel" ;
    rdfs:domain        :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range         :Framework ;
.



:isAbstract
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "isAbstract" ;
    skos:definition ''' Denotes if an element is abstract or concrete. These can be respectively
        Architecture Building Block (ABB) or Solution Building Block (SBB). SBBs are used for concrete architecture
        solutions and are normaly part of a catalogue and exists in CMDB ot ITSM solutions as well as architecture
        landscape repository. ABBs are more abstract and used for modellig patterns, reference architectures or
        hight level (superior) architectures
         ''' ;
    rdfs:domain     :Element ;
    rdfs:range      xsd:boolean ;
.

:isAbstractClass
    a               owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "isAbstractClass"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Denotes that an OWL class is abstract at the metamodel level — used for
        classification, validation, and inference via inheritance but not intended to be
        instantiated directly in architecture models. Concrete (palette-ready) classes do
        not carry this annotation.'''@en ;
    rdfs:range      xsd:boolean ;
.

:refinedInto
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "refinedInto"@en ;
    skos:definition "refinedInto"@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range      :ModelConcept ;
    owl:inverseOf   :refines ;
.

:refines
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "refines"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
        domain refines range i.e. domain is a more concrete or complete representation of range.
        This is a traceability property  intended to use within a single model or across models. I may be used as
        well as to related a particular :View that refines a :Element. For example a high level process may be
        refined by a sequence diagram.

        '''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range      :ModelConcept ;
    owl:inverseOf   :refinedInto ;
.

:partOf
    a               owl:TransitiveProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "partOf"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
    partOf is a generic property that is mainly intended for traceability and composability relations
    across models. While it can be used for composition relationships within a model, in many cases more
    specific semantics would be needed and for that new more specific relationship should be used.
    '''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range      :ModelConcept ;
    owl:inverseOf   :hasPart ;
.

:hasPart
    a               owl:TransitiveProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "hasPart"@en ;
    skos:definition "hasPart"@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range      :ModelConcept ;
    owl:inverseOf   :partOf ;
.

#################################################################
# Basic relationships
#
# Three relationship types of the core itself, for two elements no
# loaded language relates: elements of different metamodels, or of
# one that defines no relationship types. A language's own
# relationship types say more, and come first.
#################################################################

:associatedWith
    a               owl:ObjectProperty, owl:SymmetricProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "associatedWith"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Relates two model concepts that have to do with each other, where nothing more specific
is said. It has no direction: what holds from one to the other holds back.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''The weakest statement a model can make about two concepts. Use the relationship of the
modelling language where it has one for the two.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range      :ModelConcept ;
.

:Association
    a                 owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf   :QualifiedRelationship ;
    :unqualifiedForm  :associatedWith ;
    skos:prefLabel    "Association"@en ;
    skos:definition   '''A relationship between two model concepts that have to do with each other, where
nothing more specific is said; it has no direction.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote    '''A modelling tool offers it where the loaded metamodels permit no relationship between
the two elements — an element of one language and an element of another, or two of a metamodel that defines
no relationship types — and draws it as a line with no arrowhead.'''@en ;
    skos:example      '''
ex:Billing arch:associatedWith ex:LatencyRisk .
ex:Billing arch:qualifiedAssociation ex:rel-1 .
ex:rel-1 a arch:Association ;
    arch:source ex:Billing ;
    arch:target ex:LatencyRisk ;
    rdf:reifies <<( ex:Billing arch:associatedWith ex:LatencyRisk )>> .
'''@en ;
.

:qualifiedAssociation
    a                 owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel    "qualifiedAssociation"@en ;
    skos:definition   "Links the source of an Association to its relationship resource."@en ;
    rdfs:domain       :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range        :Association ;
    :unqualifiedForm  :associatedWith ;
.

:HasPart
    a                 owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf   :QualifiedRelationship ;
    :unqualifiedForm  :hasPart ;
    skos:prefLabel    "Has Part"@en ;
    skos:definition   "A relationship from a model concept to one that is a part of it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote    '''A modelling tool offers it where the loaded metamodels permit no relationship between
the two elements, and draws it from the whole to the part.'''@en ;
.

:qualifiedHasPart
    a                 owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel    "qualifiedHasPart"@en ;
    skos:definition   "Links the source of a Has Part relationship — the whole — to its relationship resource."@en ;
    rdfs:domain       :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range        :HasPart ;
    :unqualifiedForm  :hasPart ;
.

:Refines
    a                 owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf   :QualifiedRelationship ;
    :unqualifiedForm  :refines ;
    skos:prefLabel    "Refines"@en ;
    skos:definition   '''A relationship from a model concept to one it is a more concrete or more complete
representation of.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote    '''A modelling tool offers it where the loaded metamodels permit no relationship between
the two elements, and draws it from the more concrete concept to the one it refines.'''@en ;
.

:qualifiedRefines
    a                 owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel    "qualifiedRefines"@en ;
    skos:definition   "Links the source of a Refines relationship — the more concrete concept — to its relationship resource."@en ;
    rdfs:domain       :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range        :Refines ;
    :unqualifiedForm  :refines ;
.

