# reverse of viewpointGovernsView

:viewConformsToViewpoint
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "viewConformsToViewpoint"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
    viewpoint property specifies the Viewpoint individual of a particular View instance
        ''' ;
    rdfs:domain     :View ;
    rdfs:range      :Viewpoint ;
.

:modelConformsToMetamodel
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "modelConformsToMetamodel"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Model to the Metamodel (modeling language) it conforms to. A model is expressed using the vocabulary and rules defined by its metamodel. Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 "architecture description language".'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Model ;
    rdfs:range      :Metamodel ;
.

## ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
## Membership — which Model a concept belongs to, which Views present it
##
## Both properties put the *member* in the subject position. That is the
## direction the rest of this ontology already reads in — viewConformsToViewpoint,
## modelConformsToMetamodel, basedOnFramework, modelRepresentsSystem all describe
## the dependent thing rather than the container — so membership is no longer the
## one exception. There is no container-first inverse of either; see the note on
## the deprecated :contains below for why declaring one is unsound rather than
## merely redundant.
##
## Two properties rather than one, because :View is itself a :ModelConcept and a
## View may present other Views. A single container-first `contains` would render
## "this Model holds this View" and "this View presents that View" as the same
## triple pattern with a :View in object position either way, leaving no way to
## tell them apart.
##
## Neither is a subproperty of :partOf, and neither is transitive. :partOf is
## transitive composition between two ModelConcepts: presentation on a View is
## not composition, and :Model is not a :ModelConcept at all, so :inModel could
## not inherit that range even if the semantics fitted. See DD-30.
## ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

:inView
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "inView"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a ModelConcept to a View that presents it. Applies to Elements,
QualifiedRelationships and nested Views alike — anything that is a ModelConcept — and to every
kind of View: a Diagram that draws the concept, a Catalog that lists it, a Matrix that indexes
it.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Many-valued. One concept may appear in any number of Views, and appearing
in none is an ordinary state for a concept that exists in the model but has not been presented
anywhere.

A QualifiedRelationship carries this property directly, as any other ModelConcept does. No
reification is involved: the relationship is already a resource with :source and :target, so
`ex:rel1 arch:inView ex:View1` states plainly what an RDF-star annotation on an unqualified
triple would have had to state indirectly.

Independent of the visual layer, and deliberately not derived from it. Where diagram geometry
exists, core-vis says something more precise — an arch-vis:ArchNode carrying arch-vis:view and
arch-vis:archElement locates the concept on a canvas. arch:inView is the coarser fact that
survives when geometry is absent, was dropped by a serialization profile, or never existed
because the source notation had no coordinates. Both are emitted; neither substitutes for the
other.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range      :View ;
.

:inModel
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "inModel"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a ModelConcept to the Model it belongs to. Applies to Elements,
QualifiedRelationships and Views alike, so a View's membership in its Model is stated with the
same property as an Element's rather than needing a container-first term of its own.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''At most one Model per concept. That bound is asserted in core-shapes as
archsh:InModelShape rather than by typing this property owl:FunctionalProperty, which would
entail owl:sameAs between two Models instead of reporting the error. See DD-23.

Redundant with the named graph in a quad store, and deliberately so. A consumer reading
flattened Turtle or N-Triples, or a merged graph, has no other way to tell which Model a concept
belongs to short of parsing its IRI.

Named :inModel rather than partOfModel because that stem collided with :partOf, a transitive
composition property with unrelated semantics that this one neither specialises nor could
specialise.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range      :Model ;
.


## ── Superseded membership properties ──────────────────────────────────────────
## Retained so that data written against the older vocabulary still resolves, and
## declared rdfs:subPropertyOf their replacements so it still entails the current
## properties for a consumer running RDFS. Nothing in this project emits them.
## See DD-30.

:exposedInView
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:deprecated     true ;
    skos:prefLabel     "exposedInView"@en ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf :inView ;
    dcterms:isReplacedBy :inView ;
    skos:definition    '''Superseded by arch:inView, which states the same relationship under a
name that does not imply a picture.'''@en ;
    skos:historyNote   '''Renamed to arch:inView in 0.4.0. "Exposed in" reads as drawing, but
:View also covers :Catalog and :Matrix, which present concepts without depicting them. The
original definition also offered RDF-star or rdf:Statement for the relationship case; that was a
leftover from the reified design :source and :target replaced, and a QualifiedRelationship now
carries membership directly.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain        :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range         :View ;
.

:partOfModel
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:deprecated     true ;
    skos:prefLabel     "partOfModel"@en ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf :inModel ;
    dcterms:isReplacedBy :inModel ;
    skos:definition    '''Superseded by arch:inModel, which states the same relationship without
borrowing the partOf stem.'''@en ;
    skos:historyNote   '''Renamed to arch:inModel in 0.4.0. The shared stem invited the reading
that model membership is a kind of :partOf composition. It is not, and could not be made one:
:partOf is transitive with range :ModelConcept, while :Model is not a :ModelConcept.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain        :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range         :Model ;
.

:contains
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:deprecated     true ;
    skos:prefLabel     "contains"@en ;
    dcterms:isReplacedBy :inModel ;
    skos:definition    '''Superseded by arch:inModel, read in the other direction: what was
`ex:Doc arch:contains ex:OrgChart` is now `ex:OrgChart arch:inModel ex:Doc`.'''@en ;
    skos:historyNote   '''Deprecated in 0.4.0 as an undeclared inverse of what was then
:partOfModel — since :View is a :ModelConcept, both properties already stated the same fact
about a View and its Model, in opposite directions, with nothing relating them.

Carries no owl:inverseOf to :inModel, which would be unsound rather than untidy. :inModel admits
any :ModelConcept as subject while this property declares rdfs:range :View, so the inverse of
`ex:Element1 arch:inModel ex:Doc` would entail `ex:Doc arch:contains ex:Element1` and, through
that range, `ex:Element1 a arch:View`.

Carries no rdfs:subPropertyOf either, for the same reason: no container-first property here has
:inModel's breadth, so there is nothing sound to generalise to. Legacy arch:contains triples
therefore do not entail :inModel and have to be rewritten rather than reasoned over.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain        :Model ;
    rdfs:range         :View ;
.

:modelRepresentsSystem
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "modelRepresentsSystem"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Model to the System it represents. In ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010, an architecture description identifies a system of interest.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Model ;
    rdfs:range      :System ;
.


## ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
## Architecture State — Temporal Context of a Model
##
## Every architecture description exists within a temporal context:
## baseline (current state), target (desired future state), or
## transitional (an intermediate state on the path from baseline to
## target). This property indicates which temporal reality a model
## or element represents.
## ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

:ArchitectureState
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Architecture State"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The temporal context of an architecture description — whether it
represents the current state (baseline), a desired future state (target),
or an intermediate state on the migration path (transitional).'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Architecture state is orthogonal to lifecycle stage. An application
may be in lifecycle state "Run" within the baseline architecture, and
planned for lifecycle state "Retire" within the target architecture.
State classifies the model; lifecycle classifies the subject.'''@en ;
.

:architectureState
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "architecture state"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Model or Element to the temporal state it represents —
baseline (as-is), target (to-be), or transitional.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''ex:CurrentPaymentArch a arch:Model ;
    arch:architectureState arch:Baseline ;
    arch:modelRepresentsSystem ex:PaymentPlatform .

ex:TargetPaymentArch a arch:Model ;
    arch:architectureState arch:Target ;
    arch:modelRepresentsSystem ex:PaymentPlatform .'''@en ;
    rdfs:range      :ArchitectureState ;
.

:Baseline
    a               :ArchitectureState ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Baseline"@en ;
    skos:altLabel   "As-Is"@en, "Current State"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The current state of the architecture — how the system exists today.
A baseline architecture describes the implemented, operational reality.'''@en ;
.

:Target
    a               :ArchitectureState ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Target"@en ;
    skos:altLabel   "To-Be"@en, "Future State"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The desired future state of the architecture — how the system should
exist after planned changes are realised.'''@en ;
.

:Transitional
    a               :ArchitectureState ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Transitional"@en ;
    skos:altLabel   "Intermediate State"@en ;
    skos:definition '''An intermediate state between baseline and target — a stable plateau
that exists for a defined period during migration. Multiple transitional
states may exist on the path from baseline to target.'''@en ;
.

## ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
## Approval State — Governance Standing of a Model Concept
##
## Whether a concept has been through governance review and carries
## authority. Orthogonal to both lifecycle stage and architecture state:
## approval classifies the standing of the description, lifecycle
## classifies the subject, architecture state classifies the temporal
## reality the model represents.
## ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

:ApprovalState
    a               owl:Class ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Approval State"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The governance standing of a model concept — whether it has been
reviewed and endorsed by the accountable authority.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Distinct from three neighbouring vocabularies:

* ap:LifecycleStage says where the subject sits in its own life (Active, Phase Out).
  An application can be Approved and Retired at the same time.
* arch:ArchitectureState says which temporal reality a model describes
  (Baseline, Target, Transitional).
* ad:DecisionState says whether a decision *record* is settled. Approval state
  applies to the architecture element or model, not to the decision about it.

The vocabulary is deliberately open — per DD-23 there is no owl:oneOf, so a
consumer may add states its governance process needs (UnderReview, Delegated)
without editing this ontology. Constrain the permitted set in SHACL.'''@en ;
.

:approvalState
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "approval state"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a ModelConcept or Model to its governance standing.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''ex:PaymentGateway a am4:ApplicationComponent ;
    arch:approvalState arch:Approved ;
    ap:atLifecycleStage ap:Active .'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''At most one approval state applies at a time, but this is not declared
owl:FunctionalProperty — see DD-23 for why. Enforce sh:maxCount 1 in SHACL.'''@en ;
    rdfs:range      :ApprovalState ;
.

:Proposed
    a               :ApprovalState ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Proposed"@en ;
    skos:altLabel   "Submitted"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Put forward for governance review but not yet endorsed. The concept
carries no authority and may be changed or withdrawn.'''@en ;
.

:Approved
    a               :ApprovalState ;
    skos:prefLabel  "Approved"@en ;
    skos:altLabel   "Endorsed"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Reviewed and endorsed by the accountable authority. The concept is
binding for the scope it governs.'''@en ;
.

:meetsPurpose
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "meetsPurpose"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
    The purpose of the System. This is a generic property and can be anything.
        ''' ;
    rdfs:domain     :System ;
    rdfs:range      schema:Thing ;
.

#:systemInteractsWithEnvironment

:environment
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "environment"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
        System Situated In Environment which System and Environment interact some way or another.
        ''' ;
    rdfs:domain     :System ;
    rdfs:range      :Environment ;
.

:modelHasPurpose
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "modelHasPurpose"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Model to its Purpose — the reason the architecture work is undertaken.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Model ;
    rdfs:range      :Purpose ;
.

:purposeRequiresViewpoint
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  ":purposeRequiresViewpoints"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
    :purposeRequiresViewpoint is used to model the required Viewpoints when performing an architecture work with the specific
    :Purpose
        ''' ;
    rdfs:domain     :Purpose ;
    rdfs:range      :Viewpoint ;
.

:purposeRelevantViewpoint
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  ":purposeRequiresViewpoints"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
    :purposeRequiresViewpoint is used to model the relevant or recommended Viewpoints when performing an architecture work
    with the specific :Purpose
        ''' ;
    rdfs:domain     :Purpose ;
    rdfs:range      :Viewpoint ;
.


:modelAddressesConsideration
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "modelAddressesConsideration"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Model to the Considerations (concerns, aspects, perspectives) it addresses. Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Model ;
    rdfs:range      :Consideration ;
.


:metamodelIdentifiesConsideration
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "metamodelIdentifiesConsideration"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Metamodel to the Considerations it identifies as relevant for architecture descriptions using this language.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      :Consideration ;
.

:metamodelIdentifiesStakeholder
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "metamodelIdentifiesStakeholder"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Metamodel to the Stakeholders it identifies as relevant. Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      :Stakeholder ;
.

:stakeholderHasConcern
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "stakeholderHasConcern"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Stakeholder to their Concerns — the interests they have in the system and its architecture. Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Stakeholder ;
    rdfs:range      :Concern ;
.

:stakeholderHasPerspective
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "stakeholderHasPerspective"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Stakeholder to their Perspective — the viewpoint from which they consider the architecture (e.g., strategic, operational, technical).'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Stakeholder ;
    rdfs:range      :Perspective ;
.

:stakeholderHasInterestIn
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "stakeholderHasInterestIn"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Stakeholder to the System, Architecture, or Model they have an interest in. Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Stakeholder ;
    rdfs:range      :System, :Architecture, :Model ;
.

:systemHasArchitecture
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "systemHasArchitecture"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a System to its Architecture — the fundamental concepts or properties embodied in its elements, relationships, and design principles. Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :System ;
    rdfs:range      :Architecture ;
.

:modelExpressesArchitecture
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "modelExpressesArchitecture"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Model (architecture description) to the Architecture it expresses. An architecture description is a work product that expresses an architecture. Derived from ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Model ;
    rdfs:range      :Architecture ;
.


:targetsStakeholder
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "viewpointTargetsStakeholder"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
    Viewpoint property that relates targeted stakeholders. While this relationship can be infered from
    :viewpointFramesConcern and :stakeholderHasConcern it is not always the case when concerns overlap or
    there are different visual notations and representation preferences form the side of the stakeholders.
    In other words this is explicitly defined by the designer of the particular viewpoint and can match all or a
    subsets of the stakeholders. In some case it
        ''' ;
    rdfs:domain     :Viewpoint ;
    rdfs:range      :Stakeholder ;
.

:viewpointFramesConcern
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "viewpointFramesConcern"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Viewpoint to the Considerations it addresses. This is the
generic property — use the sub-properties :viewpointCoversAspect and :viewpointAddressesPerspective for
more specific queries on which structural aspect or stakeholder perspective a
viewpoint covers.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Viewpoint ;
    rdfs:range      :Consideration ;
.

:viewpointCoversAspect
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf :viewpointFramesConcern ;
    skos:prefLabel     "viewpoint covers aspect"@en ;
    skos:definition    '''Which structural aspect(s) the viewpoint covers — e.g., Active Structure,
Behavior, Passive Structure, Motivation, Composite. Values are instances of arch:Aspect.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain        :Viewpoint ;
    rdfs:range         :Aspect ;
.

:viewpointAddressesPerspective
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf :viewpointFramesConcern ;
    skos:prefLabel     "viewpoint addresses perspective"@en ;
    skos:definition    '''Which stakeholder perspective(s) the viewpoint is designed for — e.g.,
Strategic, Operational, Application, Infrastructure, Governance. Values are instances of
arch:Perspective.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain        :Viewpoint ;
    rdfs:range         :Perspective ;
.

:includesConcept
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "includesConcept"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
Defines the Concepts that may be used in this Viewpoint. These are not considered as restrictions, but can be used to
 define a palette of available Model Concepts.
 Note that more formal and rigid SHACL rules can be defined in a [shapes graph](https://w3c.github
 .io/data-shapes/shacl/#dfn-shapes-graphs)
  using property [architectureViewpointsRestrictions](#architectureViewpointsRestrictions).
            ''' ;
    rdfs:domain     :Viewpoint ;
    rdfs:range      :ModelConcept ;
.

:viewType
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "view type"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Specifies the type(s) of view a viewpoint typically produces.
Values are arch:View subclasses (arch:Diagram, arch:Catalog, arch:Matrix).'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Maps to three standard sources:

1. ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 "Model Kind" — the concept that defines conventions for
   a type of architecture model. In ISO 42010:2011, a Viewpoint specifies one
   or more Model Kinds, and each Model Kind governs Architecture Models within
   a View.

2. TOGAF Architecture Content Framework artifact classification — TOGAF classifies
   architecture artifacts into three types: Catalogs (lists of building blocks),
   Matrices (relationships between building blocks), and Diagrams (graphical
   representations). See TOGAF 10 Architecture Content, Chapter 3.

3. ArchiMate Specification (Chapter 14: Viewpoints) — ArchiMate viewpoints are
   aligned with ISO 42010. A viewpoint defines conventions for producing a view,
   which can be a diagram, catalog, matrix, or other representation of a subset
   of the architecture.

In Linked.Archi, arch:Diagram, arch:Catalog, and arch:Matrix serve as both
ISO 42010 model kinds and TOGAF/ArchiMate artifact types.

This property references OWL classes as values. Under OWL 2 Full (which
Linked.Archi effectively uses with its minimal OWL profile and SHACL-based
validation), classes can appear as property objects. No IRI is declared as
both owl:Class and owl:NamedIndividual.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Viewpoint ;
    rdfs:seeAlso    <https://www.iso.org/standard/74393.html>,
                    <https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/42010/6846/>,
                    <https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap03.html>,
                    <https://pubs.opengroup.org/architecture/archimate3-doc/ch-Stakeholders-Architecture-Views-and-Viewpoints.html> ;
.


:resultsIn
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "resultsIn"@en ;
    skos:definition "results in"@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Perspective ;
    rdfs:range      :Concern ;
.

:refinedByAspect
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "refinedByAspect"@en ;
    skos:definition "refined by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Concern ;
    rdfs:range      :Aspect ;
.

## Content dimension (Detail/Coherence/Overview) is derivable from
## viewpointCoversAspect and viewpointAddressesPerspective cardinalities.
## See skos:scopeNote on :Viewpoint class.

## Technique (e.g., tabular, graphical, matrix) is captured via
## arch:viewType which links to arch:Diagram, arch:Catalog, or arch:Matrix.

:viewpointHasPurpose
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "viewpointHasPurpose"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Viewpoint to its purpose — the type of architecture activity
it supports. Common purposes include Deciding (evaluating options, portfolio rationalization),
Designing (creating or refining architecture), Informing (communicating to stakeholders),
and Governing (compliance checking, standards enforcement).'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Distinct from :modelHasPurpose (why the architecture work exists) and
:meetsPurpose (what the system is for). This property describes what kind of activity the
viewpoint is designed to support.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Viewpoint ;
    rdfs:range      :Purpose ;
.

:prefVisNotation
    a                     owl:ObjectProperty, owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel        "preferred Visual Notation"@en ;
    skos:definition       '''The preferred (default) rendered visual notation image for a resource.

This is the "finished product" — a pre-rendered composite image (typically SVG) showing
how the concept looks in its standard visual form. Used by documentation generators,
static catalogs, and tools that need a quick icon without constructing from primitives.

For the structured construction recipe (shape + color + icon + dimensions), see the
VisualNotation class and its defaultStyle property in core-vis-onto.ttl.

The relationship between the three visual layers:
- arch:prefVisNotation → rendered image (for docs, catalogs, LLM context)
- arch-vis:VisualNotation + defaultStyle → construction recipe (for diagram editors)
- arch:usesNotationSet on PresentationContext → context-aware selection

A VisualNotation instance typically carries both: the defaultStyle (recipe) and
arch:prefVisNotation (rendered output). A tool can generate the prefVisNotation SVG
from the VisualNotation descriptor.

On a model element itself, it is that element's own picture, for every audience: a logo, a product
image. One for one audience is an arch:altVisNotation with arch:inPresentationContext. A value is the
image's own IRI, or a schema:ImageObject with its schema:contentUrl; it is for no single context.'''@en ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf    schema:image ;
    :domainIncludes       skos:Concept, :ModelConcept, <https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis#VisualNotation> ;
    schema:domainIncludes :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range            schema:ImageObject ;
    skos:broader          :PresentationCategory ;
.

:altVisNotation
    a                     owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel        "alternative Visual Notation"@en ;
    skos:definition       '''An alternative rendered visual notation image for a resource.

A resource can have multiple altVisNotation values — one per alternative
PresentationContext or NotationSet. For example, a BusinessActor might have:
- prefVisNotation → standard ArchiMate icon (for architects)
- altVisNotation → simplified flat box (for executives)
- altVisNotation → monochrome version (for printing)

When multiple notation sets exist, each VisualNotation in a non-default set
produces an altVisNotation on the concept it describes.

A picture for one audience is a schema:ImageObject with its schema:contentUrl (an xsd:anyURI: an
http(s) URL, or a data: URI of an image) and arch:inPresentationContext, the presentation context it is
for; an element has at most one per context. Without a context, a value — the image's own IRI, or an
ImageObject — is an alternative for no particular audience. How a picture for an audience is drawn on
a diagram: core-vis's arch-vis:IconCentric.'''@en ;
    skos:example          '''
## The gateway's own picture, and the one executives see.
ex:paymentGateway
    arch:prefVisNotation ex:paymentGateway-picture ;
    arch:altVisNotation  ex:paymentGateway-picture-executive .
ex:paymentGateway-picture
    a                 schema:ImageObject ;
    schema:contentUrl "https://example.org/pictures/gateway.svg"^^xsd:anyURI .
ex:paymentGateway-picture-executive
    a                          schema:ImageObject ;
    schema:contentUrl          "https://example.org/pictures/card-payments.png"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    arch:inPresentationContext am4pc:ExecutiveContext .
'''@en ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf    schema:image ;
    :domainIncludes       skos:Concept, :ModelConcept, <https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis#VisualNotation> ;
    schema:domainIncludes :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range            schema:ImageObject ;
    skos:broader          :PresentationCategory ;
.


# MetaModel Info

## eventualy using or later shapes a reference Model
:modelConcepts
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "modelConcepts"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
ModelConcepts property specifies the Ontology that defines the structure, terms, notations, semantics and basic
syntax of the modeling language. This includes Elements and Relationships subtypes.
Viewpoints are defined  using [architectureViewpoints](#architectureViewpoints) property and classified using
[viewpointLibrary](#viewpointLibrary) property. Note actual individulas Elements, Relationships, Views are defined in
 the actual Architecture Model.
        '''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      owl:Ontology ;
    skos:broader    :ModelingCategory ;
.

:architectureViewpoints
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "architectureViewpoints"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
    architectureViewpoints property specifies the ontology that defines the architecture viewpoints including
    Concerns and Stakeholders and also restriction on the viewpoints.
        '''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      owl:Ontology ;
    skos:broader    :MetamodelingCategory ;
.

:architectureViewpointsRestrictions
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf sh:shapesGraph ;
    skos:prefLabel     "architectureViewpointsRestrictions"@en ;
    skos:definition    '''
    architectureViewpoints property specifies the ontology that defines restriction on the viewpoints using SHACL or
    other method. This can also be used for SHACL processors.
        '''@en ;
    rdfs:domain        :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range         owl:Ontology ;
    skos:broader       :MetamodelingCategory ;
.

:viewpointLibrary
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "viewpointLibrary"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
    viewpointLibrary property specifies a Concept scheme that classifies available Viewpoints
        '''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      skos:ConceptScheme ;
    skos:broader    :NavigationCategory ;
.

:formalRules
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf sh:shapesGraph ;
    skos:prefLabel     "formalRules"@en ;
    skos:definition    '''
    FormalRules property specifies all the rules that a Model needs to conform to. This include Element structure,
    relationships, properties even naming conventions. A way to represent those rules can be SHACL.
    This can also be used for SHACL processors.
        '''@en ;
    rdfs:domain        :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range         owl:Ontology ;
    skos:broader       :MetamodelingCategory ;
.

:derivationRules
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf sh:shapesGraph ;
    skos:prefLabel     "derivationRules"@en ;
    skos:definition    '''
    DerivationRules property specifies the document that defines the possible derivation rules. A way to express those
    rules could be N3 notation.
    This can also be used for SHACL processors.
    '''@en ;
    rdfs:domain        :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range         owl:Ontology ;
    skos:broader       :MetamodelingCategory ;
.

:conceptClassification
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "conceptClassification"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
ConceptClassification resource is a Scheme that defines the classification of the Elements, Relationships and Viewpoints
 or other
Model Concepts defined within a Metamodel or ontology for example: `rdf:type skos:ConceptScheme , owl:Ontology` is a
consistent statement
 This classification could be used for visual aid and modelling. More than
 one classifications can exist. Classifications can be embedded within the Metamodel ontology. SKOS concept scheme(s)
  needs to be supplied.
'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      skos:ConceptScheme ;
    skos:broader    :NavigationCategory ;
.

:crossLanguageMappings
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "crossLanguageMappings"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Metamodel to a document that maps its concepts onto the concepts of
other modeling languages or frameworks, enabling queries and reports to span notations.

The mapping document is an owl:Ontology whose content is SKOS mapping assertions —
skos:exactMatch, skos:closeMatch, skos:relatedMatch, skos:broadMatch and skos:narrowMatch —
between this metamodel's classes and those of another.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''Kept separate from arch:modelConcepts because a mapping document asserts
correspondences rather than defining vocabulary; and separate from
arch:conceptClassification, which points at a SKOS ConceptScheme that classifies this
metamodel's own concepts rather than relating them to another language.

Mappings are editorial judgements, not derivations. Use skos:closeMatch rather than
skos:exactMatch unless the two concepts genuinely coincide in scope, and expect the weaker
skos:relatedMatch to be the common case across frameworks whose abstractions differ.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''togaf:TOGAFMetamodel
    arch:crossLanguageMappings <https://meta.linked.archi/togaf/crossmappings#> .

## in togaf-crossmappings.ttl
togaf:ApplicationComponent skos:closeMatch am4:ApplicationComponent .
togaf:Capability           skos:relatedMatch am4:Capability .'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      owl:Ontology ;
    skos:broader    :MetamodelingCategory ;
.

:referenceData
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "referenceData"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
any reference data that is relevant to the subject and can or should be referenced by any element or description
within the Architecture Model or the Metamodel itself.
For Reference Architectures, Models, Templates, Patterns, Tactics Reference design and implementations use
[referenceModels](#referenceModels)
'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      skos:ConceptScheme ;
    skos:broader    :NavigationCategory ;
.

:referenceModels
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "referenceModels"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
Concept scheme that contains any relevant reference Architectures, Models, Templates, Patterns, Tactics Reference
design and implementations
'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      skos:ConceptScheme ;
    skos:broader    :NavigationCategory ;
.

:standardsInformationBase
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "standardsInformationBase"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
standards concept scheme contains classification of standards and references to standards that are relevant to the
subject and can or should be used.
This scheme may also define Guidelines and Best Practices o toher informal or semi-formal guiding documentation.
Formal Patterns, Reference Architectures, Models, Building Blocks and tactics should use [Reference Architecture
Ontology](https://meta.linked.archi/ref-arch)
'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      skos:ConceptScheme ;
    skos:example    '''
* Business Standards:
    * Standard shared business functions
    * Standard role and actor definitions
    * Security and governance standards for business activity
* Data Standards:
    * Standard coding and values for data
    * Standard structures and formats for data
    * Standards for origin and ownership of data
    * Restrictions on replication and access
* Applications Standards:
    * Standard/shared applications supporting specific business functions
    * Standards for application communication and interoperation
    * Standards for access, presentation, and style
* Technology Standards;
    * Standard hardware products
    * Standard software products
    * Standards for software development
''' ;
    :mentionedIn    <https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap06.html> ;
    skos:broader    :NavigationCategory ;
.

:associatedStandard
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "associatedStandard"@en ;
    skos:definition '''
any reference to as standard that is relevant to the subject and can or should be referenced.
Any associated standard should be part of the [standardsInformationBase](#standardsInformationBase). This sometimes
require validation in that case standrad may be present in the Model but not in the Metamodel stands information
base, however, should be put in the next version of the Metamodel.

'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelConcept ;
    rdfs:range      schema:CreativeWork ;
    :mentionedIn    <https://pubs.opengroup.org/togaf-standard/architecture-content/chap06.html> ;
    skos:broader    :MetamodelingCategory ;
.


:hasDeliverableTemplate
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "hasDeliverableTemplate"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Metamodel to the DeliverableTemplates it defines. DeliverableTemplates
are part of the metamodel alongside elements, relationships, viewpoints, and rules.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      :DeliverableTemplate ;
    skos:broader    :PresentationCategory ;
    skos:historyNote "Replaces arch:hasPresentationTemplate which pointed to the now-removed arch:PresentationTemplate class."@en ;
.

:hasQuestionSet
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "hasQuestionSet"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Metamodel to the QuestionSets published for its vocabulary: the
questions its stakeholders ask of a model, bound to the concerns its viewpoints frame. A
modelling tool that loads the metamodel offers them. The core question set applies to every
metamodel and is not linked.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      :QuestionSet ;
.

## DeliverableTemplate properties

:templateRequiresViewpoint
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "templateRequiresViewpoint"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a DeliverableTemplate to the Viewpoints it presents: the model a
deliverable is produced from needs a View following each of them for the document to show it.

The sections say in which order and for which concerns (arch:templateHasSection); this flat list
lets a tool see at a glance which views a model lacks for the document.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :DeliverableTemplate ;
    rdfs:range      :Viewpoint ;
.

:templateHasSection
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "templateHasSection"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a DeliverableTemplate to its ordered TemplateSection instances.
Each section references a viewpoint and carries an order index for sequencing.
Use this property when section ordering and per-section guidance matter.
For a simpler flat list of required viewpoints, use arch:templateRequiresViewpoint.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :DeliverableTemplate ;
    rdfs:range      :TemplateSection ;
.

:sectionViewpoint
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "sectionViewpoint"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a TemplateSection to the Viewpoint it presents. Produced from a model,
the section shows the model's Views that follow this viewpoint, and the questions it asks are
pre-bound with it as ?viewpoint.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :TemplateSection ;
    rdfs:range      :Viewpoint ;
.

:sectionOrder
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "sectionOrder"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The ordinal position of a TemplateSection within its DeliverableTemplate.
Sections are ordered by ascending sectionOrder value.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :TemplateSection ;
    rdfs:range      xsd:integer ;
.

:sectionQuery
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "sectionQuery"@en ;
    skos:definition '''A SPARQL SELECT query that extracts the data needed to
populate this template section from the knowledge graph. The query is executed against
the architecture model data and its results are passed to the template engine as the
data context for this section.

Variable names in the SELECT clause become JSON keys in the template context. For example,
a query returning ?label and ?description produces objects with "label" and "description"
fields that the Handlebars template iterates over.

This property makes the deliverable generation pipeline fully declarative: a tool reads
the template sections in order, executes each section's query, and renders the template
with the results. No hardcoded extraction logic is needed.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''
:ADD-S1 arch:sectionQuery """
    PREFIX am: <https://meta.linked.archi/archimate3/onto#>
    PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>
    SELECT ?label ?description WHERE {
        ?actor a am:BusinessActor ;
               skos:prefLabel ?label .
        OPTIONAL { ?actor skos:definition ?description }
        FILTER(lang(?label) = "en")
    } ORDER BY ?label
"""^^xsd:string .
'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :TemplateSection ;
    rdfs:range      xsd:string ;
.

:sectionResultKey
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "sectionResultKey"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The JSON key under which the query results for this section are placed
in the template context. For example, if sectionResultKey is "businessActors", the
Handlebars template accesses the data via {{#each businessActors}}. If not specified,
a tool may derive the key from the section's local name or sectionOrder.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '"businessActors", "crossLayerRelationships", "requirements"'@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :TemplateSection ;
    rdfs:range      xsd:string ;
.

:deliverableTemplateUsed
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "deliverableTemplateUsed"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Model to a DeliverableTemplate its deliverables are produced with.
This lets a tool offer the document for the model, and check that the model has a View
following each viewpoint the template presents (arch:templateRequiresViewpoint).'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Model ;
    rdfs:range      :DeliverableTemplate ;
.

:templateTargetsPurpose
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "templateTargetsPurpose"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a DeliverableTemplate to the Purpose(s) it supports. Indicates
what kind of architecture activity the deliverable is designed for.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :DeliverableTemplate ;
    rdfs:range      :Purpose ;
.

:templateHasFormat
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "templateHasFormat"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a DeliverableTemplate to the PresentationFormat(s) it supports.
Specifies how the deliverable can be rendered — e.g., Markdown, HTML, PDF, slides.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :DeliverableTemplate ;
    rdfs:range      :PresentationFormat ;
.

:templateResource
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "templateResource"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Points to the template file, URL, or resource a deliverable is rendered
with: on a DeliverableTemplate, the layout of the document; on a PresentationFormat, the layout
for that format. Can be a local file path, a URL to a template repository, or a reference to a
template engine resource (e.g., Handlebars, Jinja2, XSLT).'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''
"templates/aod.md.hbs"          — Handlebars Markdown template
"templates/report.html.j2"      — Jinja2 HTML template
"templates/governance.tex"       — LaTeX PDF template
"https://example.org/templates/slides/default.pptx" — remote PPTX template
'''@en ;
    :domainIncludes :DeliverableTemplate, :PresentationFormat ;
    rdfs:range      xsd:anyURI ;
.

:generatorCommand
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "generatorCommand"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The shell command or pipeline used to produce the deliverable output
from the knowledge graph and the template resource. The command receives the
SPARQL endpoint or data file as input and produces the rendered output.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''
"handlebars render --template aod.md.hbs --data sparql://localhost:7200"
"python generate.py --format pdf --template governance.tex"
"mkdocs build --config-file mkdocs-deliverable.yml"
'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :PresentationFormat ;
    rdfs:range      xsd:string ;
.


## Model question properties — what a question is about, and what its answer means

:questionAddressesConcern
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "questionAddressesConcern"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a ModelQuestion to the Considerations its answer informs. What makes a
question selectable by concern, as arch:viewpointFramesConcern makes a viewpoint; a
stakeholder's questions follow from arch:stakeholderHasConcern.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      :Consideration ;
.

:questionTargetsStakeholder
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "questionTargetsStakeholder"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a ModelQuestion to a Stakeholder who asks it, stated where the concerns it
addresses do not already say so.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      :Stakeholder ;
.

:questionHasPurpose
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "questionHasPurpose"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a ModelQuestion to the Purposes it serves — deciding, informing, designing —
as arch:viewpointHasPurpose does a viewpoint and arch:templateTargetsPurpose a deliverable
template.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      :Purpose ;
.

:questionFitsViewpoint
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "questionFitsViewpoint"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a ModelQuestion to a Viewpoint whose views it is asked about: a modelling
tool offers it while such a view is open, and a view built from its result conforms to that
viewpoint.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      :Viewpoint ;
.

:questionStage
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "questionStage"@en ;
    skos:definition "The stage of an investigation a ModelQuestion belongs to, from arch:QuestionStageScheme."@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      skos:Concept ;
.

:answers
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "answers"@en ;
    skos:definition "What one row of a ModelQuestion's result means."@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      rdf:langString ;
.

:doesNotProve
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "doesNotProve"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The conclusion a reader will be tempted to draw from a ModelQuestion's answer and
should not — that a modelled relationship is a runtime dependency, that an absent value means
none exists.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      rdf:langString ;
.

:resultIsFinding
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "resultIsFinding"@en ;
    skos:definition '''True when every row of a ModelQuestion's result is a finding against the
concerns it addresses, so that an empty result means they are satisfied. A deliverable states
an empty finding as "none found" rather than leaving it out.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      xsd:boolean ;
.

:findingSeverity
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "findingSeverity"@en ;
    skos:definition '''How serious a finding ModelQuestion's rows are, on SHACL's scale (sh:Info,
sh:Warning, sh:Violation), so findings and validation results read alike.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      sh:Severity ;
.

:findingFocus
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "findingFocus"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The result variable, without its "?", whose value is the concept each row of
a finding ModelQuestion is about — the focus node of the finding, as in a SHACL validation result.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '"element", "service", "requirement"' ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      xsd:string ;
.

:requiresDataset
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "requiresDataset"@en ;
    skos:definition "What a ModelQuestion must be able to read: model data alone, or the metamodel beside it."@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      :DatasetScope ;
.

:requiresMetamodel
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "requiresMetamodel"@en ;
    skos:definition '''An ontology whose terms a ModelQuestion uses. A tool offers the question only while
that ontology is loaded.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      owl:Ontology ;
.

:defaultLimit
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "defaultLimit"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The number of rows a browsing tool shows of a ModelQuestion's result. Not part of
the query text: a document generator reads every row.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      xsd:integer ;
.

:resultKey
    a               owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "resultKey"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The key a ModelQuestion's rows are stored under in a document generator's context,
within the arch:sectionResultKey of the section that asks it.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :ModelQuestion ;
    rdfs:range      xsd:string ;
.

## Model question parameters
##
## The properties a ModelQuestion's sh:parameter names as its sh:path. As in SHACL,
## the variable a parameter binds is the property's local name: a parameter on
## arch:focus binds ?focus. They name inputs to questions and deliverables and
## are never asserted in model data.

:focus
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "focus"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Model question parameter: the concept a question is about — in a modelling tool,
the selected element; in a deliverable, the element it is produced for. Binds ?focus.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  "Used only as the sh:path of a question or template parameter; not asserted in model data."@en ;
.

:model
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "model"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Model question parameter: the Model a question is restricted to — in a modelling
tool, the open model; in a deliverable, the model it is produced from. Binds ?model.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  "Used only as the sh:path of a question or template parameter; not asserted in model data."@en ;
.

:view
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "view"@en ;
    skos:definition "Model question parameter: a View — in a modelling tool, the open view. Binds ?view."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  "Used only as the sh:path of a question or template parameter; not asserted in model data."@en ;
.

:viewpoint
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "viewpoint"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Model question parameter: a Viewpoint, as the statement of what is in scope — in a
modelling tool, the open view's viewpoint; in a deliverable, the section's
arch:sectionViewpoint. Binds ?viewpoint.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  "Used only as the sh:path of a question or template parameter; not asserted in model data."@en ;
.

:conceptType
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "conceptType"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Model question parameter: a class of concepts a question is restricted to, such as
one element type. Binds ?conceptType, which a question keeps apart from the ?type it may report
for each concept.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  "Used only as the sh:path of a question or template parameter; not asserted in model data."@en ;
.

:from
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "from"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Model question parameter: the earlier of two states a question compares, such as a
baseline plateau. Binds ?from.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  "Used only as the sh:path of a question or template parameter; not asserted in model data."@en ;
.

:to
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "to"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Model question parameter: the later of two states a question compares, such as a
target plateau. Binds ?to.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  "Used only as the sh:path of a question or template parameter; not asserted in model data."@en ;
.

## Template sections that answer concerns

:sectionAsks
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "sectionAsks"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The ModelQuestions a TemplateSection asks, in reading order, as an rdf:List of
arch:ModelQuestion: each defined once, asked by any number of sections and offered by any tool.
arch:sectionQuery is the inline form for a single query.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''For each question a section asks, a generator pre-binds the parameters that
question declares from the deliverable's model (?model), the section's arch:sectionViewpoint
(?viewpoint) and the arch:templateParameter values the deliverable is produced for. A question
whose required parameter stays unbound is reported as not asked; it is never run unbound.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :TemplateSection ;
    rdfs:range      rdf:List ;
.

:sectionAddressesConcern
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "sectionAddressesConcern"@en ;
    skos:definition '''A Consideration a TemplateSection answers for its readers. By default the
concerns its arch:sectionViewpoint frames; stated where the section is narrower or wider.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :TemplateSection ;
    rdfs:range      :Consideration ;
.

:sectionTargetsStakeholder
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "sectionTargetsStakeholder"@en ;
    skos:definition "A Stakeholder a TemplateSection is written for, where it differs from its viewpoint's."@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :TemplateSection ;
    rdfs:range      :Stakeholder ;
.

:templateParameter
    a                  owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf sh:parameter ;
    skos:prefLabel     "templateParameter"@en ;
    skos:definition    '''A value a deliverable is produced for — the model, the element under change,
two plateaus to compare — declared as an sh:Parameter and pre-bound into every question its
sections ask.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain        :DeliverableTemplate ;
    rdfs:range         sh:Parameter ;
.


:notationSet
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "notationSet"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Metamodel to its available NotationSet(s). A NotationSet is a named
collection of VisualNotation descriptors — one per ModelConcept — that defines the default
visual appearance for palette generation and diagram rendering.

Multiple notation sets allow alternative visual representations for the same metamodel:
standard (full spec notation), simplified (flat rectangles, no icons), monochrome
(grayscale for printing), or custom (organization-specific branding).

A palette generator reads the active NotationSet to determine shape, color, icon, and
dimensions for each element and relationship type.'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      <https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis#NotationSet> ;
    skos:broader    :PresentationCategory ;
.

:usesNotationSet
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "usesNotationSet"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a PresentationContext (a SKOS concept within a presentationContextScheme)
to the NotationSet that should be used when rendering for that audience.

This is the key property that connects "who is looking" to "how things look":
- A PresentationContext represents a stakeholder audience (Executive, Architect, Developer...)
- A NotationSet defines the visual appearance (shapes, colors, icons, line styles)
- This property bridges the two, enabling context-aware palette generation

When a tool needs to render a palette or diagram:
1. Determine the active PresentationContext (from user role or preference)
2. Follow usesNotationSet to get the appropriate NotationSet
3. Query VisualNotation instances in that set for each ModelConcept
4. Render using the defaultStyle from each VisualNotation

If a PresentationContext has no usesNotationSet, tools should fall back to the
Metamodel-level arch:notationSet (the default/standard notation).'''@en ;
    skos:example '''
am4pc:ArchitectContext
    arch:usesNotationSet am4not:ArchiMate40StandardNotation .

am4pc:ExecutiveContext
    arch:usesNotationSet am4not:ArchiMate40SimplifiedNotation .
'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:range      <https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis#NotationSet> ;
    skos:broader    :PresentationCategory ;
.

:inPresentationContext
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "inPresentationContext"@en ;
    skos:definition '''The presentation context — the audience — something is for, and only for: a name an
element is given for that audience, or what its label says on a diagram shown to it. Its object is a
concept of a presentationContextScheme; something without one is for every audience.

A name for an audience is a SKOS-XL label (skosxl:Label, its text skosxl:literalForm, language
tagged), given to the element as skosxl:altLabel — never skosxl:prefLabel: SKOS-XL makes every
skosxl:prefLabel a skos:prefLabel too, and SKOS allows one skos:prefLabel per language, which is the
element's canonical name. The label's literal form is also the element's skos:altLabel, so that
tools reading plain SKOS see it as one of its names. An element has at most one name per context and
language.

A picture for an audience is a schema:ImageObject given as arch:altVisNotation (the element's own
picture for every audience is arch:prefVisNotation); an element has at most one per context.

What a label says on a diagram is a core-vis arch-vis:LabelExpression, and how a diagram element looks
is its arch-vis:Style: one with this property applies when its context is the one presented, one without
it in every context. A diagram element has at most one style for every context and one for each context;
in a context, the latter is drawn over the former.'''@en ;
    skos:example '''
## A name for developers; the canonical name for everyone else.
ex:paymentGateway
    skos:prefLabel    "Payment gateway"@en ;
    skosxl:altLabel   ex:paymentGateway-name-developer ;
    skos:altLabel     "pgw-svc"@en .
ex:paymentGateway-name-developer
    a                          skosxl:Label ;
    skosxl:literalForm         "pgw-svc"@en ;
    arch:inPresentationContext am4pc:DeveloperContext .

## What application components' labels say for architects: the name, the type under it.
am4not:ApplicationComponentNotation
    arch-vis:labelExpression [
        a                          arch-vis:LabelExpression ;
        arch:inPresentationContext am4pc:ArchitectContext ;
        arch-vis:expression        "{name}\n«{type}»"
    ] .

## The gateway's picture for executives, and its placement drawn as it for them.
ex:paymentGateway arch:altVisNotation ex:paymentGateway-picture-executive .
ex:paymentGateway-picture-executive
    a                          schema:ImageObject ;
    schema:contentUrl          "https://example.org/pictures/card-payments.png"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    arch:inPresentationContext am4pc:ExecutiveContext .
ex:gatewayNode arch-vis:style ex:gatewayNode-style, ex:gatewayNode-style-executive .
ex:gatewayNode-style-executive
    a                          arch-vis:Style ;
    arch:inPresentationContext am4pc:ExecutiveContext ;
    arch-vis:renderingMode     arch-vis:IconCentric .
'''@en ;
    rdfs:range      skos:Concept ;
    :domainIncludes <http://www.w3.org/2008/05/skos-xl#Label>, <https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis#LabelExpression>,
                    schema:ImageObject, <https://meta.linked.archi/core-vis#Style> ;
    skos:broader    :PresentationCategory ;
.

:presentationContextScheme
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "presentationContextScheme"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Metamodel to its PresentationContext classification scheme.

A PresentationContext is a stakeholder-specific theme that determines how architecture
is visualized and communicated. Each context in the scheme can link to a NotationSet
(via arch:usesNotationSet) to specify the visual appearance appropriate for that audience.

The scheme groups contexts such as Executive, Architect, Developer, Operations, and
Governance — each representing a different audience with different information needs
and visual preferences.

Example flow for palette generation:
1. User selects a PresentationContext (e.g., "Executive")
2. Tool follows arch:usesNotationSet → gets the appropriate NotationSet
3. Tool reads VisualNotation instances from that set
4. Palette is rendered with context-appropriate shapes, colors, and detail level
    '''@en ;
    skos:example    '''
* C-Level
* Business
* DevOps
* Architects
* Infra
* Security
* Data Management
''' ;
    :rationale      '''
presentationContextScheme categories are used as 'Themes' to group those concepts and generate different
visualizations and presentation structure depending on that Context. Each context can reference a specific
NotationSet to control the visual rendering.
''' ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      skos:ConceptScheme ;
    skos:broader    :PresentationCategory ;
.

:paletteGrouping
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "paletteGrouping"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Metamodel to the classification dimensions its palette groups element types by,
the main one first: an ordered collection whose members are the dimensions, concepts of the Metamodel's
conceptClassification scheme whose narrower concepts are the groups.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''A palette makes a section of each group of the first dimension, in that dimension's order,
and puts an element type in the section of its group there; an element type with no group in it goes in the
section of its group in the next dimension. Within a section, element types follow their groups' order in the
other dimensions, then their names. A dimension's order is the ISO 25964 thesaurus array (iso-thes:ThesaurusArray,
a skos:OrderedCollection) whose iso-thes:superOrdinate is the dimension; a group the array does not list comes
after those it lists, and a dimension with no array orders its groups by name.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''
## ArchiMate 4.0: sections by domain, each in order of aspect
am4mm:ArchiMate4 arch:paletteGrouping am4tax:PaletteGrouping .
am4tax:PaletteGrouping
    a               skos:OrderedCollection ;
    skos:memberList ( am4tax:Domain am4tax:Aspect ) .
am4tax:DomainOrder
    a                      skos:OrderedCollection, iso-thes:ThesaurusArray ;
    iso-thes:superOrdinate am4tax:Domain ;
    skos:memberList        ( am4tax:CommonDomain am4tax:BusinessDomain am4tax:ApplicationDomain
                             am4tax:TechnologyDomain am4tax:StrategyDomain am4tax:MotivationDomain
                             am4tax:ImplementationAndMigrationDomain ) .
'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      skos:OrderedCollection ;
    skos:broader    :PresentationCategory ;
.

:diagramGroupOrder
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "diagramGroupOrder"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Metamodel to the groups of its conceptClassification scheme that a diagram keeps
in bands, first band first: an ordered collection of groups, which may come from different classification
dimensions.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''A layout that keeps elements in bands puts an element in the band of the first listed
group it is classified under, at any depth. An element of no listed group is placed by the elements it is
related to. The order is the arrangement a diagram of the language follows, which need not be the order of the
groups in their own dimension or in the palette.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''
## ArchiMate 4.0: motivation and strategy above the core domains; the Common Domain, used in all of them, not listed
am4mm:ArchiMate4 arch:diagramGroupOrder am4tax:DiagramGroupOrder .
am4tax:DiagramGroupOrder
    a               skos:OrderedCollection ;
    skos:memberList ( am4tax:MotivationDomain am4tax:StrategyDomain am4tax:BusinessDomain
                      am4tax:ApplicationDomain am4tax:TechnologyDomain
                      am4tax:ImplementationAndMigrationDomain ) .
'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      skos:OrderedCollection ;
    skos:broader    :PresentationCategory ;
.

:relationshipProposalOrder
    a               owl:ObjectProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel  "relationshipProposalOrder"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Links a Metamodel to an order in which a modelling tool proposes relationship types when two
elements are connected: an ordered collection of relationship types.'''@en ;
    skos:scopeNote  '''One with no :domainIncludes or :rangeIncludes is the language's general order, which applies
to every pair; a Metamodel names one. One with :domainIncludes, :rangeIncludes or both applies where the source, the
target, or both are of a class it names, or of a subclass of one: an order between behaviour elements applies to two
processes. For two elements, a tool proposes the relationships the language permits between them in three groups:
those a modeller may draw directly; then those the language permits only as the conclusion of a derivation; then
those it permits between any two elements, to use where no other fits — ArchiMate's association — unless an order
that names either element lists one. Within a group, each relationship takes the place the most specific order that
lists it gives it. An order that names both elements is more specific than one that names one, and that than the
general order; between two that name the same ends, the one whose classes are subclasses of the other's is. A
relationship's place in an order is that of its own class, or else of the first superclass the order lists: a read
access takes the place of access. So within a group the most specific order's members come first, in its order, and
what it does not list follows in the order of the next, down to the general one. Guidance, like :domainIncludes
itself: it orders what a language permits and restricts nothing.'''@en ;
    skos:example    '''
## ArchiMate 4.0: between behaviour elements, triggering and flow first; elsewhere, the strongest first
am4mm:ArchiMate4 arch:relationshipProposalOrder am4tax:ProposalOrder, am4tax:BehaviorProposalOrder .
am4tax:ProposalOrder
    a               skos:OrderedCollection ;
    skos:memberList ( am4:Composition am4:Aggregation am4:Assignment am4:Realization am4:Serving am4:Access
                      am4:Influence am4:Triggering am4:Flow am4:Specialization am4:Association ) .
am4tax:BehaviorProposalOrder
    a                   skos:OrderedCollection ;
    arch:domainIncludes am4:BehaviorElement ;
    arch:rangeIncludes  am4:BehaviorElement ;
    skos:memberList     ( am4:Triggering am4:Flow am4:Composition am4:Aggregation am4:Serving
                          am4:Specialization ) .
'''@en ;
    rdfs:domain     :Metamodel ;
    rdfs:range      skos:OrderedCollection ;
    skos:broader    :ModelingCategory ;
.

# annotation general

:laKB
    a                  owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf rdfs:seeAlso ;
    skos:definition    '''
A reference to the Linked.Archi Knowledge base section that describes this concept.'''@en ;
.

:rationale
    a                  owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf skos:note ;
    skos:definition    '''
A description of a justification of the existence of the term in that form or some other assertion or value

'''@en ;
.

:mentionedIn
    a                  owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    skos:prefLabel     "mentionedIn"@en ;
    skos:definition    '''Indicates that the concept is referred in, but is
    not necessarily the main topic.'''@en ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf rdfs:seeAlso ;
    owl:inverseOf      schema:mentions ;
.


## Ontology elemnets classification

:ModelingCategory
    a               skos:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel  "ModelingCategory"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Category for concepts used in the modeling process — elements, relationships, and views.'''@en ;
.

:PresentationCategory
    a               skos:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel  "PresentationCategory"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Category for concepts related to visual presentation — notations, templates,
rendering, and context-aware palette generation. Includes:
- NotationSet / VisualNotation (type-level visual recipes)
- prefVisNotation / altVisNotation (rendered images)
- PresentationContext → usesNotationSet (audience-driven selection)
- DeliverableTemplate / PresentationFormat (output structure)'''@en ;
.

:NavigationCategory
    a               skos:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel  "NavigationCategory"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Category for concepts used in navigation and organization — folders, catalogs, and groupings.'''@en ;
.

:MetamodelingCategory
    a               skos:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel  "MetamodelingCategory"@en ;
    skos:definition '''Category for concepts that define the modeling language itself — metamodels, viewpoints, and rules.'''@en ;
.